$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The case</h2>
<p>FIR No. 461/2025 was registered at Police Station Jyoti Nagar in September 2025 on the complaint of a minor girl, who alleged that the petitioner — a neighbour with whom she had been in contact since April 2025 — had sexual intercourse with her on several occasions, threatened her, and recorded a video. School records confirmed she was a minor, and the petitioner was charged under Sections 137(2), 64(1) and 351(2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 along with Section 6 of the POCSO Act. He was arrested in September 2025, and the sessions trial before the Special POCSO Court at Shahdara reached the prosecution-evidence stage.</p>

<h2>What weighed with the Court</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>The victim's own account</strong> — her testimony recorded at trial disclosed that she wished to marry the petitioner and that the opposition came from her family. Call records showed sustained, regular contact between the two.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Stage of evidence</strong> — the victim had been examined and cross-examined, substantially reducing the risk that liberty would translate into tampering.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Length of custody</strong> — over a year behind bars, with satisfactory conduct reflected in the nominal roll and no criminal antecedents.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Trial horizon</strong> — twenty-one prosecution witnesses meant the trial would not conclude soon; custody until judgment would be custody of indeterminate length.</div>
</div>

<h2>The adolescent-relationship jurisprudence</h2>
<p>The Court drew on the Supreme Court's recent treatment of POCSO prosecutions arising out of adolescent relationships — including its 2026 decision in <em>State of U.P. v. Anurudh</em>, which discussed the misuse of the statute against young couples and the debate over a &ldquo;Romeo-Juliet&rdquo; exception — as well as earlier Delhi High Court bail decisions in comparable fact situations. None of this dilutes the statutory position that a minor cannot consent. What it does is inform the discretion at the bail stage: where the prosecutrix's own recorded evidence speaks of a relationship rather than predation, the calculus of pre-trial detention changes.</p>

<h2>The defence and prosecution positions</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>For the petitioner</h3><p>No injuries in the medical examination, grounds of arrest not supplied, no test identification parade, and hotel records said to point away from the allegation.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>For the State and complainant</h3><p>Consistent statements of the victim, documentary links between the petitioner and the hotel, the age gap as an aggravating feature, and an early-stage trial.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The Court did not resolve these disputes — it could not, at the bail stage. It granted bail on a personal bond of Rs. 20,000 with one surety, coupled with strict distance, no-contact and reporting conditions, and preserved the State's liberty to seek cancellation on any fresh complaint.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The order is a reminder that bail in POCSO matters is not foreclosed by the gravity of the label. Where the recorded evidence suggests an adolescent relationship, the material witness has deposed, and custody has stretched past a year, courts will ask what further purpose detention serves — and will answer it with conditions rather than continued incarceration. The merits remain entirely open for the Special Court.</p>
</div>
